{"name":"Velvet Outbreak","hex":"#6b1b2a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"velvet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A closely woven fabric (originally of silk, now also of cotton or man-made fibres) with a thick short pile on one side.","Very fine fur, including the skin and fur on a deer's antlers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with velvet or with a covering of a similar texture.","To coat raw meat in starch, then in oil, preparatory to frying."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/velvet"},{"term":"outbreak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An eruption; the sudden appearance of a rash, disease, etc.","A sudden increase."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To burst out or break forth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/outbreak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Velvet","blocks":[{"text":"Velvet is a type of woven fabric with a dense, even pile that gives it a distinctive soft feel. Historically, velvet was typically made from silk. Modern velvet can be made from silk, linen, cotton, wool, synthetic fibers, silk-cotton blends, or synthetic-natural fiber blends."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velvet"},{"title":"Disease outbreak","blocks":[{"text":"In epidemiology, an outbreak is a sudden increase in occurrences of a disease when cases are in excess of normal expectancy for the location or season. It may affect a small and localized group or impact upon thousands of people across an entire continent. The number of cases varies according to the disease-causing agent, and the size and type of previous and existing exposure to the agent. Outbreaks include many epidemics, which is a term normally only used for infectious diseases, as well as diseases with an environmental origin, such as a water or foodborne disease. They may affect a region in a country or a group of countries. Pandemics are near-global disease outbreaks when multiple and various countries around the Earth are soon infected."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Difference_between_outbreak%2C_endemic%2C_epidemic_and_pandemic-en.png/330px-Difference_between_outbreak%2C_endemic%2C_epidemic_and_pandemic-en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disease_outbreak"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a9/Medici_velvet.jpg/330px-Medici_velvet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a9/Medici_velvet.jpg/330px-Medici_velvet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Velvet with Medici arms, Florence or Venice, 1440–1500","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMedici_velvet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Chasuble_abondance.JPG/500px-Chasuble_abondance.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Chasuble_abondance.JPG/1280px-Chasuble_abondance.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Chasuble_abondance.JPG/500px-Chasuble_abondance.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A cope in pile-on-pile velvet","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChasuble_abondance.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Man%27s_Coat_and_Waistcoat_LACMA_M.57.35a-b_%282_of_3%29.jpg/250px-Man%27s_Coat_and_Waistcoat_LACMA_M.57.35a-b_%282_of_3%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.w